# Split Client HTML, CSS, and JS

When writing or editing client-side web pages, keep markup, styles, and scripts in separate files. Never mix them in one file.

## Rules

- Put markup only in `.html` files
- Put styles only in `.css` files
- Put all JavaScript in `.js` files (or modules loaded from them)
- Never use inline `<style>...</style>` blocks
- Never use inline `style="..."` attributes (except when the user explicitly requires a one-off demo)
- Never use inline `<script>...</script>` blocks with code
- Never use inline event handlers (`onclick`, `onchange`, etc.)
- Link CSS with `<link rel="stylesheet" href="...">`
- Load scripts with an external `<script src="...">` tag (prefer `type="module"` when using ES modules)
- Prefer `defer` on classic scripts so the DOM is ready before they run

## Examples

```html
<!-- ❌ BAD: everything in one HTML file -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <style>
      button { color: red; }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <button style="font-size: 16px" onclick="greet()">Hi</button>
    <script>
      function greet() {
        alert("hello");
      }
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

```html
<!-- ✅ GOOD: index.html -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="./styles.css" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <button id="greet-btn">Hi</button>
    <script type="module" src="./index.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>
```

```css
/* ✅ GOOD: styles.css */
button {
  color: red;
  font-size: 16px;
}
```

```javascript
// ✅ GOOD: index.js
const greetBtn = document.querySelector("#greet-btn");
greetBtn?.addEventListener("click", () => {
  alert("hello");
});
```

When creating a new client page, create `.html`, `.css`, and `.js` files together unless the user explicitly asks for a single-file snippet.
